lesson: 192
title: A whole in three parts
level: primary-3
stars: 0
explain: When a whole is made of three parts, the parts add up to the whole. Knowing the whole and two parts, take both away.
explain: Sometimes a part is told by comparison («10 more than the first»): find that part first.
explain: 90 stickers are red, blue or green. 25 are red, and there are 10 more blue than red: 25 + 10 = 35 blue. Green: 90 - 25 - 35 = 30.
vars: a=name t=thing p=5..40 d=2..20 c=5..60
let: q = p + d
let: s = p + q + c
need: s <= 200
answer: c
exercise: {a} has {s} {t} in three bags. The first bag holds {p} {t}, and the second holds {d} more than the first. How many {t} are in the third bag?
exercise: A rope {s} m long is cut into three pieces. The first piece is {p} m long, and the second is {d} m longer than the first. How long is the third piece in metres?
exercise: A class collected {s} kg of paper in three weeks. It collected {p} kg in the first week and {d} kg more in the second week than in the first. How many kilograms did it collect in the third week?
exercise: A shop sold {s} ice creams on Friday, Saturday and Sunday. It sold {p} on Friday and {d} more on Saturday than on Friday. How many ice creams did it sell on Sunday?
exercise: {s} children went on a trip in three buses. {p} children rode in the first bus, and {d} more rode in the second bus than in the first. How many children rode in the third bus?
test: {a}'s book has {s} pages. {a.He} read {p} pages on Monday and {d} pages more than that on Tuesday. How many pages are still to be read?
test: A farmer keeps {s} birds: hens, ducks and geese. There are {p} hens, and the ducks outnumber the hens by {d}. How many geese are there?
test: Three numbers add up to {s}. The first is {p}, and the second is {d} greater than the first. What is the third number?
test: A cyclist rode {s} km in three days. On the second day the cyclist rode {q} km, which was {d} km more than on the first day. How many kilometres were ridden on the third day?
exercise: {a} has {s} {t} altogether, in three boxes. Box one has {p}. Box two has {d} more than box one. How many {t} are in box three?
exercise: How many {t} are in the third jar, if three jars hold {s} {t} in total, the first holds {p}, and the second holds {d} more than the first?
exercise: A bakery in Ohio baked {s} muffins in three batches. The first batch had {p} muffins, and the second had {d} more than the first. How many muffins were in the third batch?
exercise: Three parts make {s}. The first part is {p}. The second part is {d} more than the first. Find the third part.
exercise: {a} walked {s} km on a three-day hike in Wales. {a.He} walked {p} km on the first day and {d} km more on the second day than on the first. How many kilometres did {a.he} walk on the third day?
exercise: A library in Alberta lent {s} books over three days. It lent {p} on the first day and {d} more on the second day than on the first. How many books did it lend on the third day?
exercise: {s} people watched a school play over three nights in Brisbane. {p} came on the first night, and {d} more than that came on the second night. How many came on the third night?
exercise: A fruit stall sold {s} kg of apples, pears and plums. It sold {p} kg of apples and {d} kg more pears than apples. How many kilograms of plums did it sell?
test: Of the {s} {t} in {a}'s collection, {p} are in the first album, the second album holds {d} more than the first, and the rest are in the third album. How many {t} are in the third album?
test: A road crew in Nevada repaired {s} m of road in three days: {p} m on day one and {d} m more than that on day two. How many metres were repaired on day three?
test: Three classes in a school in Dublin raised {s} euros. Class A raised {p} euros, and class B raised {d} euros more than class A. How many euros did class C raise?
test: How many pupils go to the third team, if {s} pupils are split into three teams, the first team gets {p} pupils, and the second gets {d} more pupils than the first?
test: {a} saved {s} dollars over three months. In the second month {a.he} saved {q} dollars, which was {d} dollars more than in the first month. How much did {a.he} save in the third month?
test: A farm in Canterbury, New Zealand, has {s} sheep in three fields. The second field has {d} more sheep than the first, and the first has {p}. How many sheep are in the third field?
